# ouroboros#96: the per-iteration loop check (stall / cap) sits on the
# BACK-EDGE, after the body — compiler.c emits
#   loop_start: cond; JUMP_IF_FALSE exit; body; POP; CHECK exit; JUMP loop_start
# Emitting it before the body instead cost exactly one body execution
# (t31: steps=99 vs the VM's 100).
#
# `continue` is the second half of that contract and the reason placement
# cannot simply be "somewhere in the loop": the VM's continue_target is
# loop_start, which is AHEAD of the check, so a continued iteration skips
# it. Emitting the check last makes C's `continue` do the same thing for
# free — this program fails if the AOT ever hoists it back to the top.

# x is observed EVERY iteration and never moves, so the trajectory stays quiet
# and the loop ends through the STALL CHECK rather than through the condition —
# which is what makes the check's position observable at all. A third of the
# iterations then `continue` past it.
#
# The skips counter increments under `unobserved:` deliberately: a bare
# predicate reads the LAST-OBSERVED binding, so an observed `skips is skips + 1`
# would re-point `diverging` at a growing counter and the loop would exit
# through the condition instead — which is exactly how the first draft of this
# test managed to pass with the bug still in.
steps is 0
skips is 0
loop while not diverging:
    steps is steps + 1
    x is 5.0
    if (steps % 3) == 0:
        unobserved:
            skips is skips + 1
        continue
print of ("steps=" + (str of steps))
print of ("skips=" + (str of skips))
